## Success Criteria

The world is successful when a future AI session can generate new stills, videos, campaign ideas, and interactive scenes that feel like they belong to the same property universe without re-explaining the style from scratch, and when storyboard production can start from a clean handoff without bloating this skill.

## Current Strategic Image Expansion Overrides

These rules preserve the current successful core image generation behavior and add a smarter supplemental image layer.

- First create the normal coherent project image set requested by the user: exterior, arrival, interiors, amenities, rooftop, pool, living room, kitchen, rooms, facade, or other core spaces.
- Do not replace core project images with experimental transition images. Creative/technical images are supplemental assets.
- Use `references/strategic-image-expansion-patterns.md` when the project will become video, storyboard, social content, portfolio content, client presentation, or when the user provides real client assets.
- After the core image set, suggest or generate extra strategic images only when they add value: moodboards, details, location, loteamento, timelapse, exploded views, technical drawings, start/end frames, process images, map/route reveals, material overlays, close-ups, lifestyle proof, or other creative image types.
- The examples are not exhaustive. Think like a creative director: propose image types that make the video more attention-grabbing, professional, clear, and commercially useful while preserving project consistency.
- Every video-oriented project must identify a strong opening visual hook. The first image/shot should create curiosity, impact, transformation, or desire, not just be a beautiful render.
- Start/end frames are optional production assets. Use them only when requested or when they clearly improve transitions, timelapse, loteamento progression, before/after, technical reveal, or chained video continuity.
- For real client projects, treat uploaded renders, SketchUp screenshots, photos, plans, drone images, or construction images as architectural truth. Preserve geometry, materials, floor count, openings, furniture, site, views, and scale.
- Clearly separate what is known from client references from what is inferred or conceptual.

Updated output behavior:

- Include a `Core Image Set` first.
- Include an `Optional Strategic Supplemental Image Set` second when useful.
- Explain the purpose of each supplemental image: client-facing, social-facing, portfolio-facing, or production-facing.
- Prepare a handoff so `archviz-storyboard` can use approved core images and supplemental images without losing consistency.

